<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 48–50 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Apostolic Council</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, or Apostolic Council, is a meeting described in chapter 15 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> and held in Jerusalem about 48–50 CE. According to Acts, it addressed whether Gentile converts had to be circumcised and keep the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, a question raised by the mission of Paul and Barnabas. It decided that they did not, but told them to abstain from food associated with idols, from blood and strangled meat, and from porneia; these instructions are known as the Apostolic Decree. Scholars increasingly affirm the council as historical, but disagree on whether Galatians 2 describes the same event.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>According to Acts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> was the first centre of the Christian Church, and James the Just, the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, led the early community there. Barnabas and Paul went to Jerusalem to meet the Pillars of the Church: James, Peter and John. At the time most followers of Jesus were Jewish by birth, and even converts would have regarded the early Christians as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. Scholars hold that these Jewish Christians affirmed every aspect of Second Temple Judaism, with the addition of the belief that Jesus was the Messiah. Circumcision, the sign of the covenant with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, was disdained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Greco-Roman world</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The purpose, according to Acts, was to resolve a disagreement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. Men who had come down from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> were preaching that those not circumcised according to the custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> could not be saved, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a> who had become believers said it was needful to make Gentiles keep the law of Moses. The dispute lay between those who followed the Pillars, led by James, who thought the church must observe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, and Paul, who called himself the Apostle to the Gentiles and held that faith in Christ was sufficient.</p><p>After advice from Peter, Barnabas and Paul gave an account of their ministry among the Gentiles, and James quoted the prophet Amos. He then proposed that Gentile converts not be burdened but be told to abstain from food polluted by idols, from sexual immorality, from the meat of strangled animals and from blood. The assembly accepted this, and a letter in those terms was written. The Western textual tradition of Acts adds the negative form of the Golden Rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Apostolic Decree was binding on Christian congregations in other regions. Its purpose, scope and origin are debated. Bruce Metzger's summary of research around 1994 judged the fourfold decree original, covering food offered to idols, strangled meat, blood and unchastity, with the shorter threefold forms explained as variants; Jacques Dupont wrote that scholarship was practically unanimous in reading its prescriptions as ritual rather than ethical. According to the 19th-century German Catholic bishop Karl Josef von Hefele, the decree had been obsolete in the West for centuries.</p><p>Most scholars identify the council of Acts 15 with the meeting Paul describes in Galatians 2:1–10, and recent scholarship inclines towards treating the council and its rulings as historical, though some scholars of the mid to late 20th century were sceptical.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
